  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday Night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1113 AM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nSnow will have plenty of time to melt on Friday afternoon with \ntemperatures in the 40s, but we take a quick dive back into the \n20s overnight into Saturday morning. This will create a flash \nfreeze scenario with all the melted snow re-freezing into black \nice Saturday morning. Travel impacts will linger long beyond the \nactual frozen precip. \n\nIn the extended forecast, another low pressure system and\nattendant cold front is slated to move through Middle TN on \nSunday. This will bring another high chance (>90%) of rain to the\nentire region from Sunday morning to Sunday evening. Early \nindications of parameter space suggest minimal instability during \nthe frontal passage. RRFS maximizes SBCAPE around 100 J/kg on the \nPlateau Sunday evening, which is enough to get a few rumbles of \nthunder, but not severe weather. It's a very well-sheared \nenvironment, so any increases in instability will need to be \nwatched carefully. Will be monitoring the evolution of this next \nsystem closely over the next few days.\n\nMoving into next week, we're looking at dry and progressively\nwarmer conditions Monday through at least Thursday morning.\nTemperatures creep up to almost 10 degrees above normal in the mid\n60s by Thursday afternoon. Another shot of rain is on tap late\nweek.",
